reims-vgpu: import guest RAM as dma-bufs cut from its memfd, where a host pointer is refused.

Guest RAM that the guest channel's vhost-user device also maps is a memfd
mapped shared. radv/amdgpu import a host pointer only over private anonymous
memory: measured on an RX 6900 XT with Mesa 26.2.5, vkAllocateMemory refuses
2 GiB of shared anonymous memory, of a memfd mapped shared or private and of a
/dev/shm file with ERROR_INVALID_EXTERNAL_HANDLE, after
vkGetMemoryHostPointerPropertiesEXT accepted the pointer. So every RAMBlock
import failed and every render Store copied on the CPU.

REIMS_VGPU_GUEST_IMPORT_DMA_BUF=on asks for a second route. Where the device
advertises VK_KHR_external_memory_fd and VK_EXT_external_memory_dma_buf,
reports the dma-buf handle type importable for guest-import usage, and
/dev/udmabuf opens, the route arms (vk_caps guest_dma_buf=armed). The import
span then narrows to the kernel's udmabuf.size_limit_mb (64 MiB by default),
because one dma-buf can be no larger. Each RAMBlock window finds the memfd
behind its address from /proc/self/maps and /proc/self/fd, requires the shrink
seal udmabuf needs, cuts the window with UDMABUF_CREATE, and imports it with
VkImportMemoryFdInfoKHR. The bytes are the guest's own pages. A window that
refuses anywhere logs host_ram_import_dma_buf_refused with the check and goes
to the host-pointer route, and a refusal there to the copying rails, as before.

dma-buf windows are not warmed at the handshake: udmabuf pins every page it
covers, and warming would allocate and pin the whole of guest RAM. A window is
cut on the first reference into it.
